    "### 🔍 Understanding High Gender Violations\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why you might see 1200+ gender violations:**\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **Most Likely Cause: Severe Gender Imbalance**\n",
    "If your dataset has a major gender imbalance (e.g., 80% male, 20% female), it's **mathematically impossible** to avoid violations.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example Scenario:**\n",
    "- Tutorial group has 25 students: 20 males, 5 females\n",
    "- Creating teams of 5: Need 5 teams\n",
    "- **Problem:** You only have 5 females total, but need gender balance across 5 teams\n",
    "- **Result:** Most teams will have 4 males + 1 female = 80% male = VIOLATION\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **The 50% Rule Explanation:**\n",
    "- Our algorithm tries to keep each team ≤50% of any single gender\n",
    "- Team of 5: Maximum 2 males OR 2 females (50% = 2.5, rounded down to 2)\n",
    "- Team of 6: Maximum 3 males OR 3 females\n",
    "- **But:** If 80% of students are male, most teams will have 3-4 males = violations\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **What the Numbers Tell You:**\n",
    "- **1200 violations out of ~1200 teams = ~100% violation rate**\n",
    "- This suggests **extreme gender imbalance** in your data\n",
    "- The algorithm is doing the best it can with impossible constraints\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **Solutions to Consider:**\n",
    "1. **Accept the reality:** Some datasets simply cannot achieve perfect balance\n",
    "2. **Adjust the rule:** Maybe allow 60% or 70% instead of 50%\n",
    "3. **Focus on school diversity:** If gender balance is impossible, prioritize other factors\n",
    "4. **Report honestly:** Document the data limitations in your findings\n",
    "\n",
    "#### **This is NOT a bug** - it's the algorithm correctly identifying that your data has constraints that make perfect gender balance impossible!"
   ]
